# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# role-radar — environment template.  Copy to `.env`.
#
# This file holds ONLY machine-level config: where your profile lives, and API
# credentials. Everything about *you* — target titles, locations, salary band,
# keywords, CSV column mapping, research depth — lives in
# `$PROFILE_DIR/profile.json`. Start from `profiles/_template.json`.
#
# WHY THE SPLIT MATTERS: this file used to carry 34 further keys (TARGET_ROLES,
# PREFERRED_LOCATIONS, MIN_KEYWORD_SCORE, CSV_COL_*, RESEARCH_DEPTH, …) that were
# inherited from the single-user repo and that NO code in this repo reads. A new
# user could fill in every one of them carefully and have all of it silently
# ignored — a config surface that looks authoritative and is decorative. They are
# gone. If a key is not in this file, it is not read.
#
# Check your setup:  uv run python -m src.jobcore.profile validate
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ── 1. WHERE YOUR PERSONAL DATA LIVES (the one required key) ────────────────
# Absolute path to a directory OUTSIDE this repo, holding profile.json, your CV,
# and your pipeline. Keeping it outside the repo is what lets two people share
# this platform without sharing their job searches.
#
# Windows + Git Bash: write the WINDOWS form (C:/Users/you/job-search), not
# /c/Users/... . The MSYS form is translated, but the native form is unambiguous —
# an unresolvable path used to fall back to defaults silently, scoring everything
# flat with no error.
PROFILE_DIR=C:/Users/you/job-search

# Only used for the PDF filename, and only when the CV has no `# ` H1 to read the
# name from. The CV's H1 wins.
YOUR_NAME=


# ── 2. LLM PROVIDER (the headless jobcore lane ONLY) ────────────────────────
# TWO LAYERS, DIFFERENT COST — know which one you are paying for:
#
#   • Skills layer (research / CV tailoring / gates — the judgment) runs INSIDE
#     Claude Code and is paid by your Claude subscription, NOT by anything below.
#     Even headless: `claude -p "..."` via a one-time OAuth token
#     (`claude setup-token` -> CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN), no API key.
#     Do NOT proxy the skills to another vendor — tool-use and subagents degrade
#     20-40% and extended thinking is Anthropic-only.
#
#   • jobcore headless layer (`src/jobcore/llm.py` — fit notes, classification)
#     -> THIS section. Point it at cheap or free models.
#
# Default is Ollama: local, free, no vendor lock.
LLM_PROVIDER=ollama            # ollama | anthropic | openai | openrouter

# Option A: Ollama (local, free, private) — DEFAULT
#   Linux/Windows: docker compose up -d ollama   |   Mac: brew install ollama && ollama serve
#   Then: ollama pull llama3.1:8b   (Mac/Apple Silicon: qwen2.5:7b is a good small default)
OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11434
OLLAMA_MODEL=llama3.1:8b

# Option B: Anthropic API (pay-as-you-go; NOT your Claude subscription)
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5

# Option C: any OpenAI-compatible host (OpenAI, vLLM, LM Studio, Together)
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-...
# OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# GPT-5.x / o-series need `max_completion_tokens` instead of `max_tokens`; the adapter
# switches on model name automatically. Provider error bodies are surfaced verbatim, and a
# reasoning model's empty `content` falls back to its reasoning field.

# Option D: OpenRouter — cheapest cutover for this layer. One gateway to
# DeepSeek / GPT-5.x / Llama / Qwen; swap models with one line.
# `LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter` auto-defaults the base URL, so you need not set it.
# If you DO set it yourself it must end at /api (the adapter appends
# /v1/chat/completions) — https://openrouter.ai/api, NOT .../api/v1.
# LLM_PROVIDER=openrouter
# OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-or-...
# OPENAI_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-chat     # append :free on some models for a free tier
# Attribution headers — some free-tier models rank or gate on them:
# OPENROUTER_REFERER=
# OPENROUTER_TITLE=role-radar

# ── Fallback chain (optional — quota / vendor-lock insurance) ───────────────
# Ordered provider hops, tried until one answers. ANY failure of a hop (429, auth,
# 5xx, timeout, connection, or an empty reply) advances to the next — so a run
# finishes even when a free lane is exhausted.
# Syntax: comma/newline/semicolon separated `provider[:model]`. Only the FIRST
# colon splits provider from model, so model names keep their own colons.
# LLM_FALLBACK_CHAIN=ollama:qwen2.5-coder:3b, openrouter:qwen/qwen3-coder:free, openrouter:deepseek/deepseek-chat
# Unset = use the single LLM_PROVIDER above.

# Verify a lane before you rely on it (e.g. on a new machine):
#   uv run python -m src.jobcore.smoke                    # probe every hop in the chain
#   uv run python -m src.jobcore.llm "Reply with: OK"     # the configured provider


# ── 3. EXA SEARCH (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Neural web search via the Exa MCP server — Tier 3 of `job-radar deep`. Without
# it the ATS and LinkedIn tiers still run; you just lose hidden/careers-only roles.
# Its real value is routing AROUND login-walled and SPA pages (Glassdoor, company
# /careers) to syndication mirrors. Fetching a walled URL directly fails either way.
#
# The free tier works with NO key. Set one only for higher rate limits:
#   dashboard.exa.ai -> API Keys
# Copy .mcp.json.example to .mcp.json, then run /mcp in Claude Code to connect.
# The key is read from here, so it stays out of .mcp.json and out of git.
# EXA_API_KEY=exa_...
